                    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                        To calculate the instantaneous weight gain rate of MD2 Pineapple, we differentiate the Logistic Growth function using the *Chain Rule* and *Quotient Rule*:
                    </p>
                    <div class="bg-slate-50 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-250 font-mono text-xs text-slate-700 space-y-2">
                        <p class="font-bold">1. Define the original logistic equation:</p>
                        <p class="text-emerald-700">f(t) = L * [1 + e^(-k(t-t0))]^(-1)</p>
                        <p class="font-bold">2. Apply Chain Rule where u = 1 + e^(-k(t-t0)):</p>
                        <p>f'(t) = -L * [1 + e^(-k(t-t0))]^(-2) * d/dt [1 + e^(-k(t-t0))]</p>
                        <p class="font-bold">3. Differentiate internal exponential term:</p>
                        <p>d/dt [1 + e^(-k(t-t0))] = -k * e^(-k(t-t0))</p>
                        <p class="font-bold">4. Substitute and simplify:</p>
                        <p class="text-amber-700 font-bold">f'(t) = (L * k * e^(-k(t-t0))) / (1 + e^(-k(t-t0)))^2</p>
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                        Notice how the derivative can also be expressed recursively as: 
                        <span class="code-font font-semibold bg-emerald-50 text-emerald-800 px-2 py-0.5 rounded">f'(t) = k * f(t) * (1 - f(t)/L)</span>. 
                        This teaches us that rate of weight gain is proportional to the size already achieved, limited by the resource carrying capacity of the plant.
                    </p>
